/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ctrl10_brine_concentration  --  the dynamic path carrying an ELECTROLYTE
                                  package, which nothing in the corpus did.

  A stirred tank fed a concentrated NaCl brine, held on temperature by a
  jacket loop.  There is NO reaction: the vessel is a mixing dynamic, a
  first-order lag on the holdup, which is the honest thing to model when the
  point is the THERMODYNAMICS travelling through the transient rather than
  kinetics.

  Why it exists.  Speciation was a steady-state property of this project by
  accident, not by decision: choupoSolve grew the two-bases contract, batch
  got it on 2026-07-31, and choupoCtrl had never seen an electrolyte package
  at all -- so nobody could say whether the contract held there.  It builds
  the same ThermoPackage as the other two binaries and writes per-species
  material at every accepted step; this case is what makes that testable
  instead of assumed.

  What a student sees: the jacket loop settling a brine whose activity
  coefficients are Pitzer's, not ideal -- and the run saying so in its own
  banner.  The material at every face is the apparent basis (water, NaCl),
  the same basis the steady and batch cases carry.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoCtrl;
description   "Dynamic brine tank on a Pitzer package: the electrolyte thermodynamics through a transient";

verbosity     3;

startTime       0;        // s
endTime         600;      // s
deltaT          1.0;      // s
writeInterval   50;       // s
